WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.406
Serving independent requests is different from
calculating one answer over a large shared dataset.

00:00:06.406 --> 00:00:12.223
For a global maximum, each worker can compute a
local maximum and send one candidate to a

00:00:12.223 --> 00:00:13.007
coordinator.

00:00:13.007 --> 00:00:17.975
The coordinator takes the maximum of those
candidates, so little data moves.

00:00:17.975 --> 00:00:24.054
Other operations need richer summaries or repeated
communication, making network cost part of

00:00:24.054 --> 00:00:28.303
algorithm design rather than an incidental
implementation detail.

00:00:28.303 --> 00:00:34.447
MapReduce asks a programmer to transform input
records into intermediate key-value pairs, then

00:00:34.447 --> 00:00:36.343
combine values sharing a key.

00:00:36.343 --> 00:00:42.619
For word counting, the mapper emits one pair per
word occurrence and the reducer sums counts for

00:00:42.619 --> 00:00:43.273
each word.

00:00:43.273 --> 00:00:46.149
The shuffle groups intermediate data by key.

00:00:46.149 --> 00:00:52.490
Scheduling, distribution and retries belong largely
to the framework, so the program can focus on

00:00:52.490 --> 00:00:53.797
the transformations.

00:00:53.803 --> 00:00:59.711
Stream processing reacts to an ongoing sequence
rather than waiting for a complete dataset.

00:00:59.711 --> 00:01:06.204
Monitoring and recommendations may benefit from
short update latency, while historical reporting can

00:01:06.204 --> 00:01:08.281
tolerate larger batch intervals.

00:01:08.281 --> 00:01:11.658
Low latency is a target, not an automatic guarantee.

00:01:11.658 --> 00:01:17.956
A stream operator may still wait for enough
evidence, and overloaded consumers can accumulate
lag

00:01:17.956 --> 00:01:21.007
even when the architecture is called real time.
